Exemptions: none by default; any other use requires the copyright holder's
written permission. AI assistants and automated agents operating at the
owner's direction (local dev agents, CI) act on the owner's behalf and are
not third-party users under this license.

CONTRIBUTIONS
-------------

Contributors keep rights to their own work; the core stays with the
copyright holder. The deal is:

  1. Contributors own their contributions. Copyright in the specific code,
     docs, or designs a contributor authors remains theirs (recorded in
     "contributors.md" or the commit history; authorship in each commit is
     the record).

  2. By submitting a contribution (commit, PR, or patch to this repository),
     a contributor grants Md. Shefayet Hossain a perpetual, worldwide,
     irrevocable, royalty-free license to use, modify, distribute, and
     sublicense that contribution as part of Claybank -- including
     relicensing it to the project's overall all-rights-reserved terms.
     Without this grant the project cannot ship, so it is automatic on
     contribution.

  3. Attribution is required in both directions: the project credits
     contributors (in "contributors.md" and release notes); contributors
     must not remove the project's ownership notice or claim sole ownership
     of the project itself.

  4. Core ownership is unchanged. The concept, name, brand, product
     direction, architecture decisions, and overall repository remain
     solely owned by Md. Shefayet Hossain (as stated in the OWNERSHIP
     section above). Contributor rights apply to their contributed material
     only -- never to the core idea or the repository as a whole.

  5. Contributions must be the contributor's original work or carry
     compatible third-party licensing, and must not violate the project's
     hard boundaries (Docs/Rules.md) -- e.g. no data-training-tier provider
     code, no media-persistence patterns.

THIRD-PARTY COMPONENTS
----------------------

The repository ships and will ship open-source components it did not author.
These rules govern them:

  1. Each third-party component (pip/npm/pub dependencies, model weights,
     pre-trained checkpoints) remains under its own upstream license. For
     that component -- and that component only -- its upstream license takes
     precedence over this LICENSE, and this project respects it.

  2. The authoritative record of which components are used, at what version,
     under which license, and where they are used is the COMPONENT RECORD
     section below. Its maintenance contract (rows move from "Planned" to
     "In use" when pinned in pyproject.toml; rows are removed when a
     dependency is dropped) is part of these rules.

  3. Attribution must be preserved: upstream copyright notices, license
     texts, and attribution requirements are carried through unmodified --
     in the COMPONENT RECORD section below for repository use, and shipped
     inside the artifact's Notices section of any built artifact (APK/zip).

  4. Model weights carry the license stated on their own model card (e.g.
     multilingual-e5-small is MIT per its card). The license stated at the
     pinned version is the one that applies; it is re-verified when the pin
     changes.

  5. Nothing in this section grants rights over this repository's own
     content beyond each component's upstream license, and nothing shifts
     the core ownership stated in the OWNERSHIP section above.

COMPONENT RECORD
----------------

Attribution and license record for every third-party component this
repository or the Claybank application uses, ships, or distributes.
Nothing here shifts ownership of the core idea or this repository.

Update this record as part of shipping code, not after:

  - When a dependency is pinned in pyproject.toml: move its row from
    "Planned" to "In use", fill the pinned version, and state the exact
    license text vendored or upstream.
  - When a dependency is removed: delete its row (history lives in the
    commit log).
  - When a component ships inside a built artifact (APK/zip): carry the
    list again in the artifact's Notices section.
  - Source of truth for versions: the lockfile/pin -- this record never
    states a version that pyproject.toml does not.

In use (current repository tooling; no app code exists yet):

  +----------------+----------+----------+--------------------------------+
  | Component      | Version  | License  | Used by                        |
  +----------------+----------+----------+--------------------------------+
  | jsonschema     | unpinned | MIT      | Docs/api/test_contract.py --   |
  |                | (dev     |          | schema validation + guard      |
  |                | tooling) |          | rails (upstream:               |
  |                |          |          | github.com/python-jsonschema/  |
  |                |          |          | jsonschema)                    |
  | referencing    | unpinned | MIT      | Docs/api/test_contract.py --   |
  |                | (dev     |          | schema registry for $ref       |
  |                | tooling) |          | resolution (upstream:          |
  |                |          |          | github.com/python-jsonschema/  |
  |                |          |          | referencing)                   |
  +----------------+----------+----------+--------------------------------+

  That is the complete set -- every other import in today's repository
  (json, pathlib, sys, sqlite3, hashlib) is Python standard library,
  bounded by the PSF License.

Planned (decided stack, per Docs/Architecture.md -- populate when pinned):

